  "HANDCUFFS (character `cuffed: true`, or `cuffed: { from: seconds }`): a cuffed figure's hands stay together behind its back in every move — the arms never swing, it balances with its shoulders and body (a little more lean, shorter slower steps); it can struggle (tug) but the hands stay together. The Handcuffs are WORN, not held: each cuff is a closed metal band around a wrist (the forearm passes through it — from the side a narrow band across the wrist just above the hand, with its hinge bump), the two side by side, joined by a very short chain, turning with the wrists.",
  "POLICE ESCORT (escort, partner = the cuffed one): the police walks right behind in step, one hand holding the cuffs, the other on the shoulder. AN ESCAPE ATTEMPT IS SUDDEN AND UNPLANNED-LOOKING: in the middle of walking (walk, tug, walk — never a stop or a wind-up first) the prisoner lunges to break free. A REACTION ALWAYS COMES AFTER ITS CAUSE: the police didn't expect it, so it reacts about 0.2 s later — grips tight, digs its heels in, stops and pulls the prisoner back — never the other way round; then they walk on. They only stop because of the escape attempt. The escort follows by itself.",
  "MOVING BACKGROUNDS: rain falls slanted by the wind and splashes; trees and grass bend in the wind (foot still, top most, more in gusts) and lose a leaf now and then; a waterfall pours in bands, foams, mists and ripples its pool. Smooth every picture; raindrops and leaves are symbols. Mix pieces into originals: a thunderstorm = sky `time: \"storm\"` + rain + trees/grass with `wind` + lightning.",
  "BACKGROUNDS FILL THE PAGE: sky, hills, ground, water and weather always cover the whole page and reach past its edges, on any page shape, when the scene is shifted or zoomed out, and through camera cuts — never cut off before the edge.",
  "EXPLOSIONS (explosion; Arthur: \"the app doesn't know what an explosion is\"): a GROUND explosion (a grenade, not a nuke) is a quick bright FLASH and a FIREBALL at the ground, then a STEM rising with a big CLOUD on top like a small mushroom (almost a tree shape) — wavy layered gray-brown smoke — with dirt streaks and pebbles thrown out that fall and land, a low dust skirt that stays along the ground, a thin dim light on the ground and a scorch mark. FAST, THEN SLOW (Arthur): it grows abnormally fast from the flash (the stem and cloud at full size in about 0.2 s — keep the impact picture: the bright red/orange/yellow fireball with debris and dirt streaks), snaps to a stop — but smoke never freezes: after the stop it KEEPS GROWING SLOWLY the whole time until it is gone, at a slow-walk pace (the cap about 0.15 figure heights a second taller, the cloud about 0.1 wider), its edges billowing and the stem swaying a little every picture — and it breaks up and fades together, gone about 3 seconds after the stop; twice the size you'd guess (a grenade's cloud about 2.5 figure heights up). A ground blast has a thick stem — one solid flat gray with bumpy edges, no light line down it (that looks like road markings) — that widens very slightly upward under a big cloud; dust spikes jet out of the EDGE of the ground cloud with a clear gap from the stem (never at its base, never in it): usually 2, sometimes 3, rarely 4 (bigger blasts → more spikes), every one pointing AWAY from the blast point, random sizes and spacing (most half a figure tall, now and then one taller than a figure); an air blast bursts equally in all directions; flying debris (planks, chips, pebbles) are symbols. When the stem disintegrates it breaks along IRREGULAR cut lines (different heights, tilted, jagged) into chunks of varied shapes — one roughly a block, one more like a triangle, one roundish — with wobbly edges, never even horizontal slices or stacked squares. No two explosions are the same; the ground spikes are rough, uneven and see-through. HOT SPOTS: only 2–3 per explosion, each ONE flat color (orange OR yellow, picked per spot; never red), each a tear IN one gray cloud piece — inside it, drifting and wobbling a little in it from the heat, never its own floating cloud. The tearing starts right AT the explosion: as soon as the cloud shows, its blobs are already tearing open — thin cracks that open slowly, little by little (never just appearing), while the gray thins away; they shrink back a little. A spot SHARES ITS PIECE'S FADE exactly: its alpha = its own x its piece's alpha every picture (as see-through as its gray piece), and both are gone at the same moment. A small, short screen shake on the blast. Things that BREAK (a wooden crate) break apart: a blast throws pieces of whatever it breaks up into the air in arcs, like it throws people — closer and lighter pieces fly higher and farther, then fall, bounce and settle (crateBreak: Wood plank and Wood chip symbols, never below the ground). HOW FAR IT PUSHES depends on its strength and the distance: a stronger blast knocks people back from farther away (close and strong = blownAway; far or weak = only a flinch); light things fly farther than people. WHERE IT GOES OFF DECIDES ITS SHAPE (Arthur, Oct 7: \"an explosion in the air explodes evenly on all sides; on the ground it goes up — you can't explode downward\"): on the GROUND it can only go UP (the ground stops it): a stem and a mushroom cloud; in the AIR (nothing around it) it bursts EVENLY in all directions: a round fireball and a LUMPY, overlapping mass of smoke clumps of different sizes at uneven angles (never a neat ring of petals — \"no sunflowers, that's physically impossible\"), sparks thrown every way at uneven angles, no stem (explosion `air`); a big blast is FELT: the whole picture jolts HARD for just 2 pictures — the impact frames — then stops dead (screen shake `impact`); words out of an air burst come straight out of its middle.",
  "THROWING: overhand to throw far (the wind-up behind the head, step in, follow-through), underhand for a short toss (the arm swings back low, step in, release forward and up at hip height, follow-through up). Even a short toss is a real throw with an arc — never straight up and down. THE EFFORT MATCHES THE FLIGHT: the object leaves at the hand's speed and direction — a hard throw goes far, a soft toss stays short; a light object is thrown with little effort, like passing a basketball (throw `effort` above 1 = a stronger, faster throw; `loft: true` lets go while the hand still rises — a taller arc: overhand about 40 degrees up, peaking at least half a figure above the hand). NO SLOW MOTION: every action runs at its natural speed — a light or short throw is quick and easy, never slowed down; reuse the passed move for the same action (an underhand toss is the passed underhand pass: the same rhythm, every beat as long). A short toss keeps the natural quick rhythm with a smaller swing (throw `swing` below 1: a short back-swing, a smaller follow-through) — distance comes from the size of the swing, never from slowing down. A held thing (a grenade) is a symbol in the hand until it leaves it, then it flies from exactly where the hand let go.",
  "BOUNCING: a thrown thing that hits the ground BOUNCES, each bounce lower than the last (the ground takes most of the fall and slows it down), then ROLLS a little, slowing, turning as it rolls, and SETTLES. A grenade explodes only after it has settled and a short pause (its fuse).",
