  "WEAPON FIGHTS: the weapon does the hitting — alternating slashes left/right (an X), overhead chops, thrusts, spin attacks, dash → airborne, both hands on it → slash on landing; blocks/parries with the weapon; kicks/punches/pushes only ~10%. The weapon is a symbol held along the forearm; its hitting part must really reach. Open strong, something every second or so, every attack answered on its hit, end clearly. Keep fighting distance like any fight (never inside each other; close in, the guard goes up out of the way); crossed swords last a moment, then they break apart; a parry meets the blade in front of the body; reuse the passed fight moves (dash, hits, reactions); between exchanges they close in at once and the next wind-up starts on the way in — never standing face to face; both fighters fight: a strike is met by a swing, and a parry is answered by a counter — trading blows, never just blocking; a strike after a big wind-up carries the body's momentum — a heavy clash, never a tap; blades meet on the upper half of the blade, never at the handle; a spin attack keeps both hands on the grip with the blade out in front, sweeping round with the body (the hands moving along the front of the chest at each turn of the view, never jumping across it) — never lifted overhead unless asked; a full turn reads as ONE WAY ROUND, never a 180 and back: what is held out in front traces a loop (dipping toward us on the near side, rising on the far side) and on the far side it is drawn BEHIND the body; a block puts the upper half of its blade in the attack's path with the hands out of it (low for a high attack) — blades never meet at the handle, and the attacker aims at that upper half, never at the hands; against a rising slash the blade leans out at the attacker so its upper half is met first; in every picture of the playing animation (wind-up, swing, contact, follow-through) a blade stays ≥ 0.15 h from the other's hands and handle; a dodged strike reaches with its tip only to where the body was; a dash/jump strike with a weapon is POWERFUL: take off from the run's last stride about one dash-length away, cock it high behind the head in the air, start the downswing in the last part of the flight so it ARRIVES AS THE FRONT FOOT PLANTS (contact = touch-down, never after — a late swing reads as a tap), a big arc, the body driving in (lean in, hips drop with the landing), a 2-picture hit stop at 12 fps, the other pushed back, then follow through; a weapon dash flies ~0.5 s at full speed, then lands heavy and stops fast; A HEAVY STRIKE'S ENERGY GOES SOMEWHERE: no pause on the contact — the spark flies and the weapon keeps moving, carrying on through and DOWN (blocked: the hands draw in first so it never slides down onto the other's hands), slows to a stop pointing down, then comes back up slowly into the guard (never a tap that bounces straight back up); a heavy blow drives the block back AT ONCE — knocked back off its feet in a short hop, landing still guarding, then a beat to recover; a spin's turn is quick (slow coil, fast turn, then the slash); after the opener the fighters stand at striking distance and exchange a strike about every second — no walking in and out between strikes (reach with the lunge and lean; one small step only if the gap really drifts; a block rocks the weight back, no steps).",
